{"module":"conquest","voice":"female","duration":255.4,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Discovery and conquest","start":0.0,"end":1.94,"words":[[0.05,0.84],[0.84,1.1],[1.1,1.81]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Before there was a color line in North America, there was a land line.","start":2.74,"end":7.06,"words":[[2.79,3.07],[3.07,3.29],[3.29,3.5],[3.5,3.64],[3.64,3.93],[3.93,4.21],[4.21,4.35],[4.35,4.57],[4.57,5.14],[5.38,5.6],[5.6,5.81],[5.81,5.95],[5.95,6.24],[6.24,6.59]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The idea that Christian Europeans could claim lands already lived on became U.S. law, and every Native nation lived with the consequences.","start":7.41,"end":16.21,"words":[[7.47,7.61],[7.61,7.96],[7.96,8.17],[8.17,8.73],[8.73,9.44],[9.44,9.65],[9.65,10.0],[10.0,10.35],[10.35,10.77],[10.77,11.05],[11.05,11.19],[11.19,11.61],[11.61,11.97],[12.32,12.53],[12.77,12.99],[12.99,13.27],[13.27,13.69],[13.69,14.11],[14.11,14.39],[14.39,14.6],[14.6,14.74],[14.74,15.66]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Nations, not wilderness","start":16.56,"end":18.76,"words":[[16.61,17.31],[17.62,17.88],[17.88,18.58]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Hundreds of nations with their own governments, trade networks and cities lived here.","start":19.46,"end":24.42,"words":[[19.46,19.99],[19.99,20.12],[20.12,20.58],[20.58,20.78],[20.78,20.98],[20.98,21.17],[21.17,21.89],[22.12,22.45],[22.45,22.91],[22.91,23.11],[23.11,23.44],[23.44,23.7],[23.7,23.96]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Cahokia, near present-day St. Louis, had perhaps 10,000 to 20,000 people around 1100 CE.","start":24.77,"end":31.68,"words":[[24.77,25.33],[25.55,25.74],[25.74,26.37],[26.37,26.68],[26.99,27.24],[27.46,27.65],[27.65,28.03],[28.03,28.65],[28.65,28.78],[28.78,29.59],[29.59,29.91],[29.91,30.28],[30.28,31.1],[31.1,31.28]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Disease, war and enslavement followed contact; by 1900 the U.S. census counted only about 237,000 Native people.","start":32.03,"end":41.61,"words":[[32.08,32.48],[32.72,32.86],[32.86,33.06],[33.06,33.8],[33.8,34.2],[34.2,34.74],[34.98,35.18],[35.18,36.13],[36.13,36.26],[36.26,36.6],[36.94,37.34],[37.34,37.81],[37.81,38.15],[38.15,38.49],[38.49,40.31],[40.31,40.72],[40.72,41.12]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Historian Andrés Reséndez estimates that 2.5 to 5 million Native people were enslaved across the Americas between 1492 and 1900, a figure that is still debated.","start":41.96,"end":54.89,"words":[[42.0,42.7],[42.7,43.24],[43.24,43.94],[43.94,44.56],[44.56,44.79],[44.79,45.65],[45.65,45.8],[45.8,46.11],[46.11,46.58],[46.58,47.04],[47.04,47.43],[47.43,47.59],[47.59,48.21],[48.21,48.6],[48.6,48.75],[48.75,49.37],[49.37,49.84],[49.84,50.85],[50.85,51.08],[51.08,52.25],[52.52,52.67],[52.67,52.98],[52.98,53.22],[53.22,53.37],[53.37,53.68],[53.68,54.38]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Discovery becomes law; removal follows","start":55.79,"end":59.4,"words":[[55.84,56.72],[56.72,57.31],[57.31,57.6],[57.94,58.63],[58.63,59.21]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Johnson v. M'Intosh (1823) the Supreme Court held that “discovery” gave the U.S. title to Native land.","start":60.1,"end":68.08,"words":[[60.16,60.3],[60.3,60.81],[60.81,61.02],[61.38,61.88],[61.88,62.88],[63.13,63.27],[63.27,63.7],[63.7,63.92],[63.92,64.2],[64.2,64.42],[64.42,65.06],[65.06,65.35],[65.35,65.49],[65.49,65.85],[66.21,66.64],[66.64,66.78],[66.78,67.21],[67.21,67.57]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Worcester v. Georgia (1832) it ruled that Georgia had no authority over Cherokee land, but President Jackson's government enforced the Indian Removal Act (1830) anyway.","start":68.43,"end":80.77,"words":[[68.49,68.64],[68.64,69.01],[69.01,69.24],[69.61,70.07],[70.07,70.89],[71.15,71.3],[71.3,71.61],[71.61,71.83],[71.83,72.28],[72.28,72.51],[72.51,72.73],[72.73,73.26],[73.26,73.56],[73.56,74.16],[74.16,74.54],[74.8,75.02],[75.02,75.7],[75.7,76.3],[76.3,76.98],[76.98,77.43],[77.43,77.58],[77.58,78.03],[78.03,78.55],[78.55,78.78],[78.78,79.46],[79.72,80.24]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The Cherokee, Chickasaw, Choctaw, Muscogee (Creek) and Seminole nations were forced west; thousands of Cherokee died on the Trail of Tears in 1838–39.","start":81.12,"end":93.65,"words":[[81.17,81.33],[81.33,82.06],[82.34,82.98],[83.26,83.83],[84.11,84.83],[84.83,85.16],[85.44,85.68],[85.68,86.32],[86.32,86.89],[86.89,87.05],[87.05,87.37],[87.37,87.77],[88.05,88.7],[88.7,88.86],[88.86,89.5],[89.5,89.83],[89.83,89.99],[89.99,90.15],[90.15,90.55],[90.55,90.71],[90.71,91.03],[91.03,91.2],[91.2,93.13]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Some of these nations had adopted slavery, and enslaved Black people were forced along the same trails.","start":94.0,"end":100.68,"words":[[94.02,94.24],[94.24,94.4],[94.4,94.62],[94.62,95.15],[95.15,95.38],[95.38,95.91],[95.91,96.6],[96.86,97.09],[97.09,97.7],[97.7,98.0],[98.0,98.38],[98.38,98.53],[98.53,98.83],[98.83,99.14],[99.14,99.29],[99.29,99.59],[99.59,100.12]]},{"el":"h2","text":"War and extermination","start":101.58,"end":103.48,"words":[[101.63,101.82],[101.82,102.1],[102.1,103.31]]},{"el":"s2","text":"At Sand Creek, Colorado, in 1864, U.S. volunteers killed more than 150 Cheyenne and Arapaho people, most of them women, children and elders, at a camp flying a U.S. flag.","start":104.18,"end":118.15,"words":[[104.23,104.37],[104.37,104.65],[104.65,105.01],[105.25,105.96],[106.21,106.35],[106.35,107.34],[107.59,107.95],[108.3,108.94],[108.94,109.22],[109.22,109.36],[109.36,109.58],[109.58,110.78],[110.78,111.21],[111.21,111.42],[111.42,111.99],[111.99,112.41],[112.66,113.01],[113.01,113.16],[113.16,113.37],[113.37,113.79],[114.04,114.61],[114.61,114.82],[114.82,115.25],[115.49,115.64],[115.64,115.78],[115.78,116.06],[116.06,116.49],[116.49,116.63],[116.63,116.98],[117.34,117.69]]},{"el":"s2","text":"At Wounded Knee in 1890, soldiers killed about 250 or more Lakota.","start":118.5,"end":124.04,"words":[[118.56,118.7],[118.7,119.12],[119.12,119.26],[119.26,119.41],[119.41,120.25],[120.49,121.05],[121.05,121.33],[121.33,121.68],[121.68,122.8],[122.8,122.87],[122.87,123.01],[123.01,123.57]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In California, Governor Peter Burnett declared in 1851 that “a war of extermination will continue to be waged”; state-funded militias killed thousands, and the Native population fell from about 150,000 to about 30,000 between 1846 and 1870.","start":124.39,"end":142.98,"words":[[124.44,124.59],[124.59,125.33],[125.58,126.03],[126.03,126.32],[126.32,126.69],[126.69,127.2],[127.2,127.35],[127.35,128.31],[128.31,128.53],[128.53,128.67],[128.67,128.82],[128.82,128.97],[128.97,129.93],[129.93,130.15],[130.15,130.74],[130.74,130.88],[130.88,131.03],[131.03,131.55],[131.8,132.61],[132.61,133.13],[133.13,133.42],[133.42,134.08],[134.34,134.56],[134.56,134.71],[134.71,135.15],[135.15,135.96],[135.96,136.18],[136.18,136.48],[136.48,136.85],[136.85,138.61],[138.61,138.76],[138.76,139.13],[139.13,139.94],[139.94,140.38],[140.38,141.33],[141.33,141.56],[141.56,142.51]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 2019 California's governor apologized and called it genocide.","start":143.33,"end":148.15,"words":[[143.38,143.52],[143.52,144.45],[144.45,145.16],[145.16,145.58],[145.58,146.36],[146.36,146.58],[146.58,146.86],[146.86,147.0],[147.0,147.71]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Allotment and termination","start":149.05,"end":150.91,"words":[[149.1,149.74],[149.74,149.98],[149.98,150.78]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The Dawes Act (1887) broke reservations into individual plots and sold the “surplus.”","start":151.61,"end":158.03,"words":[[151.66,151.8],[151.8,152.02],[152.02,152.24],[152.24,153.28],[153.53,153.9],[153.9,154.71],[154.71,155.0],[155.0,155.81],[155.81,156.18],[156.18,156.4],[156.4,156.77],[156.77,156.92],[156.92,157.43]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Native-held land fell from about 138 million acres in 1887 to about 48 million by 1934.","start":158.38,"end":167.71,"words":[[158.43,159.21],[159.21,159.53],[159.53,159.76],[159.76,160.08],[160.08,160.47],[160.47,162.05],[162.05,162.52],[162.52,162.91],[162.91,163.07],[163.07,164.17],[164.17,164.33],[164.33,164.72],[164.72,165.35],[165.35,165.82],[165.82,166.06],[166.06,167.16]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In the 1950s, “termination” policy ended federal recognition of more than 100 tribes, and the Relocation program moved Native families to cities with promises of jobs that often didn't exist.","start":168.06,"end":182.21,"words":[[168.11,168.27],[168.27,168.43],[168.43,169.53],[169.81,170.6],[170.6,171.07],[171.07,171.47],[171.47,172.02],[172.02,172.81],[172.81,172.97],[172.97,173.13],[173.13,173.37],[173.37,174.16],[174.16,174.71],[174.99,175.22],[175.22,175.38],[175.38,176.25],[176.25,176.88],[176.88,177.2],[177.2,177.67],[177.67,178.23],[178.23,178.38],[178.38,178.78],[178.78,179.02],[179.02,179.65],[179.65,179.81],[179.81,180.2],[180.2,180.44],[180.44,180.76],[180.76,181.15],[181.15,181.7]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Boarding schools","start":183.11,"end":184.34,"words":[[183.15,183.68],[183.68,184.2]]},{"el":"s4","text":"“Kill the Indian in him, and save the man,” said Richard Henry Pratt, who founded the Carlisle Indian Industrial School in 1879.","start":185.04,"end":194.3,"words":[[185.04,185.27],[185.27,185.43],[185.43,185.89],[185.89,186.04],[186.04,186.35],[186.62,186.85],[186.85,187.16],[187.16,187.31],[187.31,187.62],[187.62,187.85],[187.85,188.31],[188.31,188.7],[188.7,189.08],[189.35,189.5],[189.5,190.04],[190.04,190.2],[190.2,190.73],[190.73,191.19],[191.19,192.04],[192.04,192.35],[192.35,192.5],[192.5,193.81]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The federal government ran or supported 408 boarding schools that took children from their families, banned their languages and religions, and used harsh discipline.","start":194.65,"end":205.84,"words":[[194.7,194.85],[194.85,195.39],[195.39,196.08],[196.08,196.31],[196.31,196.39],[196.39,196.93],[196.93,198.01],[198.01,198.39],[198.39,198.78],[198.78,199.01],[199.01,199.24],[199.24,199.86],[199.86,200.17],[200.17,200.4],[200.4,201.01],[201.28,201.59],[201.59,201.82],[201.82,202.59],[202.59,202.82],[202.82,203.59],[203.86,204.09],[204.09,204.4],[204.4,204.63],[204.63,205.33]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The Interior Department's investigation documented at least 973 deaths of children (2024).","start":206.19,"end":213.1,"words":[[206.25,206.37],[206.37,206.93],[206.93,207.56],[207.56,208.37],[208.37,209.05],[209.05,209.18],[209.18,209.42],[209.42,210.86],[210.86,211.11],[211.11,211.23],[211.23,211.73],[211.73,212.66]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In October 2024 President Biden formally apologized.","start":213.45,"end":217.67,"words":[[213.52,213.65],[213.65,214.11],[214.11,215.03],[215.03,215.63],[215.63,216.02],[216.02,216.42],[216.42,217.21]]},{"el":"h5","text":"Resistance and sovereignty today","start":218.57,"end":220.72,"words":[[218.62,219.32],[219.32,219.55],[219.55,220.24],[220.24,220.63]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Native activists occupied Alcatraz (1969–71) and Wounded Knee (1973), and won the Indian Self-Determination Act (1975).","start":221.42,"end":232.71,"words":[[221.47,221.91],[221.91,222.56],[222.56,223.14],[223.14,223.72],[223.72,225.83],[226.08,226.3],[226.3,226.73],[226.73,226.88],[226.88,228.18],[228.44,228.66],[228.66,228.87],[228.87,229.02],[229.02,229.45],[229.45,230.62],[230.62,230.83],[230.83,232.21]]},{"el":"s5","text":"In McGirt v. Oklahoma (2020) the Supreme Court held that much of eastern Oklahoma remains Muscogee (Creek) reservation for purposes of federal criminal law.","start":233.06,"end":243.16,"words":[[233.11,233.24],[233.24,233.58],[233.58,233.78],[234.12,234.79],[234.79,235.6],[235.84,235.97],[235.97,236.37],[236.37,236.58],[236.58,236.85],[236.85,237.05],[237.05,237.32],[237.32,237.45],[237.45,237.79],[237.79,238.46],[238.46,238.94],[238.94,239.54],[239.54,239.81],[240.05,240.72],[240.72,240.86],[240.86,241.33],[241.33,241.46],[241.46,241.93],[241.93,242.47],[242.47,242.68]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The protests at Standing Rock (2016) drew thousands of supporters, and in 2021 Deb Haaland (Laguna Pueblo) became the first Native American cabinet secretary.","start":243.51,"end":254.55,"words":[[243.56,243.7],[243.7,244.32],[244.32,244.46],[244.46,244.94],[244.94,245.15],[245.15,246.05],[246.3,246.5],[246.5,247.06],[247.06,247.2],[247.2,247.75],[248.0,248.2],[248.2,248.34],[248.34,249.38],[249.38,249.59],[249.59,250.01],[250.01,250.42],[250.42,250.91],[251.15,251.57],[251.57,251.71],[251.71,251.98],[251.98,252.4],[252.4,252.96],[252.96,253.44],[253.44,254.06]]}]}